You are given a DLL of size N consisting of positive integers.
The task is to reverse the list and return the new head 

Node structure:

class Node {
public: 
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* back; //Pointer to previous node 

    Node(int data1, Node* nex1 = nullptr, Node* back1 = nullptr) {
        data = data1;
        next = next1;
        back = back1;
    }
};

Brute force solution using Stack:
1. Handle Base Cases: 
If head is nullptr or head->next is nullptr (0 or 1 node)
return head directly 

2. First Pass (Store Data): traverse the dll using a temp pointer and push each nodes data onto an auxiliaru stack<int>

3. Second Pass: (Overwrite Values): Reset temp back to head. Traverse the list again pop the top element form the stack, overwrite temp->data with it, and move temp to temp->next

Return Head: Return head (the node pointers remain unchanged) but the values are reversed 


Node* reverseDLLBruteForce(Node* head) {
    //Corner case: list is empty or has only one node 
    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {
        return head;
    } 

    stack<int> st;
    Node* temp = head;

    //Step1: Push all values in stack
    while (temp != nullptr) {
        st.push(temp->data);
        temp = temp->next;
    }

    //Step 2: Pop values from stack and reassign to nodes 
    temp = head;
    while(temp != nullptr) {
        temp->data = st.top();
        st.pop();
        temp = temp->next;
    }

    return head;

}

TC: O(2N)
We need two passes one to populate the stack and one to write the values back taking O(N) time.

SC: O(N) 
Extra memeory is allocated for stack storing N nodes.

2. Optimal Solution 
1. Handle base case: If head == nullptr and head->next == nullptr return head 

2. Initialize the pointers: Create a pointer current initialized to head and pointer last initialized to nullptr

3. traverse and swap: While curr is not nullptr:
    Save the forward pointer curr->next in temp variable.
    Swap the current nodes pointer: set curr->next to curr->back and curr->back to temp 
    Update the last = curr to track the last visited node (which will end up being the new head)
    Move curr forward in the original list order by setting curr = temp.

4. Return the new head: Return last which now points to the head of the reversed list.

Node* reverseDLLOPtimal(Node* head) {
    //Corner case: list is empty or has 1 node 
    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr) {
        return head;
    }

    Node* curr = head;
    Node* last = nullptr;

    while (curr != nullptr) {
        //Preserve the next pointer before swapping 
        Node* temp = curr->next;

        //Swap next and back pointers 
        curr->next = curr->back;
        curr->back = temp;

        //Save the current references as last visited node 
        last = curr;

        //Move the next node in original sequence 
        curr = temp;
    }

    //Return new head of reversed DLL
    return last;
}

TC: O(N)
Single linear pass traversing all N nodes 

SC: O(1)
Done completely in-place by updating pointer references without allocating memory 